Against a fake that echoes the headers it received, each request shows up carrying the same `billing-sync/1.4 (python-client)` user-agent and its own request id — req-0001, req-0002, req-0003 — one per call.

Every request your client makes should carry two headers, and the echo in this run shows what the vendor sees when it does:

- **`X-Request-Id`** — a unique id *you* generate per request. This is the answer to support's question: when something goes wrong, you quote `req-0002`, and the vendor greps their logs for exactly that request — its arrival time, the account it resolved to, the response it produced. Without it, the conversation is "it failed sometime Tuesday," and everyone is sad. (Generate it per *request*, not per job: retries get fresh ids, and you log each attempt's id so the vendor can see the retry sequence too. A UUID in production; the counter here keeps the demo readable.)
- **`User-Agent`** — who is calling: application name, version, and client library (`billing-sync/1.4`). Vendors use it to distinguish your traffic from every other integration on your account, to warn *you specifically* when you're using a deprecated pattern, and to not ban your IP when a different tenant of the same API misbehaves.

The client-side habits that complete the picture: log the request id with every response and every error (it's the join key between your logs and theirs), include it in any error you surface to your own support channel, and keep the UA version honest — bump it when the client changes behavior, because "all the failing traffic is billing-sync/1.3" is a sentence that ends incidents.

